       The return values of these macros are undefined (i.e., they should  not
       be used as the right-hand side of assignment statements).

NNOOTTEESS
       All  of these interfaces are macros.  A “&” is not necessary before the
       variables _y and _x.

PPOORRTTAABBIILLIITTYY
       The ggeettyyxx, ggeettppaarryyxx, ggeettbbeeggyyxx and  ggeettmmaaxxyyxx  macros  are  described  in
       X/Open Curses, Issue 4.

       This  implementation also provides functions ggeettbbeeggxx, ggeettbbeeggyy, ggeettccuurrxx,
       ggeettccuurryy, ggeettmmaaxxxx, ggeettmmaaxxyy, ggeettppaarrxx and ggeettppaarryy for  compatibility  with
       older versions of _c_u_r_s_e_s; see ccuurrss__lleeggaaccyy(3X).

       Although X/Open Curses does not address this, many implementations pro‐
       vide members of the WWIINNDDOOWW structure containing values corresponding to
       these  macros.   For best portability, do not rely on using the data in
       WWIINNDDOOWW, since some implementations make WWIINNDDOOWW opaque (do not allow di‐
       rect use of its members).

       Besides the problem of opaque structures, the data stored in like-named
       members may not have like-values in different implementations.  For ex‐
       ample,  the  WWIINNDDOOWW..__mmaaxxxx  and  WWIINNDDOOWW..__mmaaxxyy values in _n_c_u_r_s_e_s have (at
       least since release 1.8.1) differed by one from some other  implementa‐
       tions.  The difference is hidden by means of the macro ggeettmmaaxxyyxx.

